{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## LIVE CONFIRMATION of the `missing` gap, from tonight's outage\n\nEarlier I recorded that `rebar-gerrit-ec2-instance-check` and `rebar-gerrit-ec2-system-check`\nare set to `treat_missing_data = \"missing\"` — a THIRD value this ticket's original sweep never\nenumerated, because it partitioned the world into `breaching` vs `notBreaching`. I flagged the\nconsequence as theoretical: a stopped or unreachable instance would produce NO alarm.\n\n**Tonight it stopped being theoretical.**\n\nAt 21:28 UTC (2026-09-04) the Gerrit host stopped serving entirely — HTTP 000 connection timeouts\non every endpoint, all `rebar/host` metric publishing ceased. Ten alarms correctly fired between\n21:48 and 21:54 UTC as their dead-man windows filled:\n\n  rebar-gerrit-gate-down, rebar-mcp-serving-path-down, rebar-root-disk-pressure,\n  rebar-gerrit-data-disk-high, rebar-gerrit-data-disk-debris, rebar-gerrit-g2p-dispatch-errors,\n  rebar-gerrit-replication-errors, rebar-gerrit-voter-errors,\n  rebar-review-bot-merge-change-errors, rebar-gerrit-mirror-out-of-sync\n\n**Neither EC2 status-check alarm was among them.** Through a complete host outage, the two alarms\nwhose entire purpose is to report instance health stayed silent. After the recovery stop/start\nthey read `INSUFFICIENT_DATA`.\n\n## What this does and does not prove\n\nIt PROVES the `missing` setting produces silence during exactly the event class the alarms exist\nfor. It does NOT prove those two alarms are useless — the outage was detected anyway, within seven\nminutes, by ten correctly-configured `breaching` alarms. This ticket's own fix is what caught it.\n\nSo the honest framing: the EC2 checks contributed NOTHING to detecting a real outage, while the\nremediated `rebar/host` alarms did all the work. That is an argument for finishing the sweep\nacross all values of `treat_missing_data`, not an argument that detection is broken.\n\n## Bearing on the ticket\n\nStrengthens the recommendation already recorded here: if the sweep is re-run or codified as a\ncheck, it must ENUMERATE ALL VALUES of `treat_missing_data`, not just the two it was originally\nlooking for. An audit that partitions on `breaching`/`notBreaching` will keep reporting clean\nover a third state it never asks about — and tonight shows that third state is not hypothetical.\n\nAC3 (production fault-injection) remains operator-reserved. Note, though, that tonight was an\nUNPLANNED natural experiment covering part of what AC3 would test: it demonstrated that the\nremediated alarms genuinely reach ALARM on a real publisher death, which is the property AC3\nexists to verify. It does not substitute for AC3 — nobody induced it, and it exercised only the\ndead-publisher path, not a fetch/REST failure."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1788560001469167001,"uuid":"638c4d89-d41e-44bf-b7b9-c35862283657"}